e collateral consequences, including whether there is disproportionate harm to shareholders, pension holders, employees, and others not proven person-ally culpable, as well as impact on the public arising from the prosecution;

e the adequacy of the prosecution of individuals responsible for the corporation's malfeasance; and

e the adequacy of remedies such as civil or regulatory enforcement actions.

As these factors illustrate, in many investigations it will be appropriate for a prosecutor to consider a corpora-tion's pre-indictment conduct, including voluntary disclo- sure, cooperation, and remediation, in determining whether to seek an indictment. In assessing a corporation's coopera-tion, prosecutors are prohibited from requesting attorney-

